    "caption": "Platinum is white without any plating. White gold is an alloy, usually coated in rhodium that wears off, while platinum is naturally white and needs no coating. Jewelry platinum is usually about 95 percent pure, stamped 950, and jewelers like it for prongs because it is strong and wears slowly. It is denser than gold, so a platinum ring feels heavier, and when it scratches the metal mostly gets pushed aside, leaving a soft patina a jeweler can polish bright. Pure platinum melts near 1,768 degrees Celsius, so casting needs special equipment. The renders in this video were made with HeyGema.",
